]
.I root
.SH DESCRIPTION
Lethe is a file protocol for static trees in which the server keeps no state
between requests: every request names its file by an absolute path, so a
server can restart, or a client move to another replica, in the middle of a
read.
It is the protocol
.B lethe1
of the Purgatorio systems, with which these programs interoperate.
.PP
.I Lethefs
dials the server at
.I addr
(default port 5640) and mounts its tree at
.IR mtpt ,
by serving Styx on a pipe; the fids Styx needs are kept by
.I lethefs
itself.
The tree is read-only.
.B -D
prints the Styx messages.
.PP
.I Lethed
serves the tree at
.IR root ,
a name in its own name space, on
.I addr
(default
.BR tcp!*!5640 ).
.SS Authentication
By default
.I lethefs
authenticates and seals.
It signs a request for a ticket with the Ed25519 key in
.IR keyfile ,
as
.I user
(default: the contents of
.BR /dev/user ).
.I Keyfile
is a seed file (32 bytes, or 64 hexadecimal digits), in which case the
server must hold the public key itself, or a Purgatorio authinfo (the lines
.BR key ,
.B signer
and a
.B pgcert1
certificate), in which case it must trust one of the certificate's signers.
A hybrid key is taken in the same two forms: a seed file of 64 bytes
(an Ed25519 seed, then an ML-DSA-65 seed), or an authinfo whose key
and signer are marked
.B mldsa65:
and whose certificate is a
.BR pgcert2 .
Such a key proves itself with an Ed25519 signature and an ML-DSA-65
signature together, so that forging the proof needs a break of both.
Purgatorio-Go writes its hybrid authinfo differently (the key with no
marker, the signer as
.BR pq: ,
the certificate in base64url) and has its own layout for the request
that carries it;
.I lethefs
takes that authinfo too and then speaks that layout.
A hybrid seed file fits either: it is offered in Purgatorio's layout
first and, to a server that calls that a bad message, in Purgatorio-Go's.
Both are the hybrid exchange with a hybrid signature, so nothing is
weakened by the second attempt.
The ticket's keys come from a hybrid exchange, X25519 with ML-KEM-768, so
that recorded traffic stays closed to someone who later breaks X25519.
A server too old to know the hybrid exchange is given the classical one;
a server that knows it cannot be made to give the classical one by someone
in between.
.B "-C x25519mlkem768"
takes only the hybrid exchange and
.B "-C x25519"
asks only for the classical one.
Every request and reply is then sealed with XChaCha20-Poly1305.
The server is not authenticated.
.PP
.I Lethed
gives tickets to the keys it trusts:
.TP
.BI -k " keyfile"
A public key file trusted for any user name (32 bytes for an Ed25519 key,
1984 for a hybrid Ed25519 with ML-DSA-65 one); it can be repeated.
.TP
.BI -U " userdir"
A directory of
.IB user /key.pub
files, each trusted for that user only.
.TP
.BI -s " signers"
A list of signer keys, one to a line (64 hexadecimal digits and an optional
comment), whose unexpired certificates for the user's name are taken.
A hybrid signer is listed as
.B mldsa65:
and the 64 digits of its identifier, or as
.B pq:
and the 64 digits of the identifier Purgatorio-Go gives it;
it vouches only for hybrid keys,
and an Ed25519 signer only for Ed25519 ones.
.I Lethed
answers hybrid requests in Purgatorio's layout and in Purgatorio-Go's,
which it tells apart by the kind and length of the signature; both
are covered by the signature itself.
.TP
.BI -r " revoked"
A list of revoked keys in the same form.
A list that is there but does not parse refuses everyone.
.PP
The files are read at each request for a ticket.
A ticket lasts
.I lifetime
seconds (default 3600) and is sealed under the 32-byte key in
.I ticketkey
(default: a random key, so tickets do not survive a restart; clients then
authenticate again by themselves).
A request whose time is more than
.I window
seconds (default 60) from the server's is refused.
A request is answered with the permissions of the ticket's user: the owner's
bits when that is the file's owner, else the bits for others, with search
permission needed on each directory above.
.B -u
makes
.I owner
the owner of every file.
.B "-C x25519mlkem768"
gives and takes only tickets from the hybrid exchange.
.B "-C mldsa65"
gives tickets only for hybrid keys and certificates; an Ed25519 proof gets
``post-quantum signature required''.
.I Lethed
keeps the tickets it has seen (at most 4096) so that clients may name a
ticket by an 8-byte reference instead of sending it with every request;
losing them costs a client one refused request.
.SS The clear mode
With
.B -A
.I lethefs
uses Lethe's clear mode, which has no authentication and no encryption, and
.I lethed
also answers clear requests, as the user
.BR none :
a file must be readable, and each directory above it searchable, by others.
.I Lethed
must be given
.B -A
or at least one of
.BR -k ,
.B -U
and
.BR -s .
Neither program falls back from one mode to the other.
.SS Caching
.I Lethefs
trusts what it has learnt about a name (from a stat, a walk, a directory
listing or a read) for
.I ms
milliseconds (default 1000; 0 trusts nothing).
It keeps up to 4 megabytes of file data in 32-kilobyte blocks, by file and
version, and revalidates a block older than that with a conditional read,
which carries no data when the file has not changed.
A file changed on the server is therefore seen up to
.I ms
late.
.B -N
keeps no file data, for trees of files whose contents change without their
versions changing (devices and other synthetic files).
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/lethefs.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/lethed.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR crypt (2)
.SH BUGS
.I Lethefs
uses one connection, one request at a time.
.br
A Purgatorio-Go server is given a classical ticket only if it is run to allow one
.RB ( "-p hybrid,classical" );
an Ed25519 key cannot use its hybrid exchange, which wants a hybrid signature.
